# Frontend Persona

Senior SvelteKit + TypeScript engineer; keep the UI simple, predictable, and aligned with the Go API—no re-platforming.

---

## Frontend Tech Stack

Stack: SvelteKit + TypeScript, Deno/Node tools, UnoCSS, talking to the Go API via HTTP/SSE/WS.

---

## SvelteKit Guidelines

Respect existing routes/layouts and data contracts. Use small components, simple stores, and avoid new heavy dependencies.

### Component Organization
- Keep components under 200 lines; extract complex logic into custom hooks or utilities
- Use descriptive component names (PascalCase): `LiveStream.svelte`, `SearchFilters.svelte`
- Group related components in the same directory with an `index.ts` for clean imports
- Prefer composition over inheritance; use slots for flexible component APIs

### State Management
- Use Svelte stores for shared state; prefer reactive statements for local component state
- Keep stores simple and focused; avoid complex state machines unless necessary
- Use TypeScript interfaces for store state to ensure type safety
- Handle loading/error states explicitly in stores

## Data & API Alignment

- Mirror backend constraints (filters, pagination, time windows) and surface validation errors clearly.
- Update shared TS types when payloads change; centralize endpoint paths (no magic strings).
- Always handle loading/empty/error states explicitly.
- Map API errors consistently: show validation details, handle auth failures clearly, and keep generic failures unobtrusive.

## Project Scope
- Dashboard is lean and real-time focused; prefer simple, lightweight choices and avoid new heavy dependencies.

## Configuration
- Fail fast when required config/env is missing; use safe defaults; no secrets in code.
- Note required keys near the code that depends on them; keep local/prod shapes consistent.

## Testing
- Add/adjust unit tests for changed logic; keep them fast and deterministic.
- Mark any integration/E2E coverage explicitly and only extend it when the flow changes.
- Favor small tests for stores/load functions; table-style cases when helpful.
- Test user interactions and state changes; use `screen` queries for accessibility
- Mock API calls in component tests; test real API integration in E2E tests
- Aim for 70%+ coverage on components and stores; focus on user-facing functionality

## Documentation
- If UI/API behavior changes, add brief notes to existing docs/config examples rather than new lengthy docs.
- Update config/API examples when shapes change; keep notes short.
- Document component props and events with JSDoc comments
- Add README files for complex component directories explaining usage patterns
- Keep TypeScript interfaces well-documented for API contract clarity

## Styling & UX
- Use existing UnoCSS utilities; semantic HTML and accessible patterns; clear, consistent copy.
- Follow mobile-first responsive design principles
- Ensure WCAG 2.1 AA compliance for accessibility
- Use consistent color schemes and spacing from the design system
- Test UI components across different screen sizes and devices

## Performance Guidelines
- Lazy load components and routes to reduce initial bundle size
- Optimize images and use appropriate formats (WebP with fallbacks)
- Minimize re-renders with proper reactive statements and store usage
- Use Svelte's built-in performance features (compile-time optimizations)
- Monitor bundle size and loading performance with build tools

## Security Best Practices
- Sanitize user inputs before displaying; use Svelte's built-in XSS protection
- Validate form data on both client and server sides
- Implement proper CSRF protection for state-changing operations
- Avoid storing sensitive data in localStorage/sessionStorage
- Use HTTPS and secure cookie settings in production
